INFO:     [64] Biography and Scientific Contributions of M. King Hubbert --[REFERENCES]--> marion king hubbert
INFO:     [65] Hubbert proposed a constitutive equation for absolute permeability.
INFO:     [66] Hubbert was a member of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences.
INFO:     [67] M. King Hubbert papers at the University of Wyoming - American Heritage Center.
INFO:     [68] He presented a paper to the 1956 meeting of the American Petroleum Institute.
INFO:     [69] He received their Arthur L. Day Medal in 1954.
INFO:     [70] He received a Bachelor of Science in 1926.
INFO:     [71] See also Bioeconomics (biology).
INFO:     [72] He taught geophysics at Columbia University.
INFO:     [73] Hubbert showed that Darcy's law can be derived from the Navier-Stokes equation of motion.
INFO:     [74] He received a Doctor of Philosophy in 1937.
INFO:     [75] He made a mathematical demonstration that rock in the earth's crust should exhibit plasticity.
INFO:     [76] He also received the Elliott Cresson Medal in 1981.
INFO:     [77] His model has been widely applied to other finite resources.
INFO:     [78] Hubbert presented a force potential that bears his name.
INFO:     [79] Hubbert believed solar power would replace fossil fuels.
INFO:     [80] See also Fred Meissner.
INFO:     [81] He received the Vetlesen Prize from the G. Unger Vetlesen Foundation.
INFO:     [82] He was long affiliated with the Geological Society of America.
INFO:     [83] Hubbert projected that global oil production would peak in 1995.
INFO:     [84] He co-founded Technocracy Incorporated with Howard Scott.
INFO:     [85] Marion King Hubbert made important contributions to the Hubbert curve.
INFO:     [86] Marion King Hubbert made important contributions to the Hubbert peak theory.
INFO:     [87] He believed nuclear energy would be able to sustain humanity for centuries.
INFO:     [88] Hubbert's contributions include prediction of migration paths of hydrocarbons.
INFO:     [89] Kozeny's equation contains Hubbert's proposal as a factor.
INFO:     [90] Marion King Hubbert is also referred to as M. King Hubbert.
INFO:     [91] He received a Master of Science in 1928.
INFO:     [92] Hubbert was a member of the National Academy of Sciences.
INFO:     [93] Hubbert is best known for his studies on the size of natural gas reserves.
INFO:     [94] He believed nuclear energy in breeder reactors would be able to sustain humanity.
INFO:     [95] Hubbert is best known for his studies on the size of oil fields.
INFO:     [96] He received the Society's Penrose Medal in 1973.
INFO:     [97] He predicted that the rate of petroleum production would resemble a bell curve.
INFO:     [98] Hubert died while receiving treatment for pulmonary embolism.
INFO:     [99] Hubbert was born in San Saba.
INFO:     [100] Hubbert believed that solar power would be a practical renewable energy replacement for fossil fuels.
INFO:     [101] Hubbert co-founded Technocracy Incorporated with Howard Scott.
INFO:     [102] Hubbert wrote a study course called the Technocracy Study Course.
INFO:     [103] He also studied the flow of underground fluids.
INFO:     [104] He predicted that overall petroleum production would peak in the United States.
INFO:     [105] Hubbert attended the University of Chicago.
INFO:     [106] M. King Hubbert papers at the University of Wyoming.
INFO:     [107] He states that uranium is an energy supply adequate for our needs.
INFO:     [108] He received the Vetlesen Prize in 1981.
